# Line endings. Why this file exists.
#
# `.prettierrc.json` sets `"endOfLine": "lf"`, so `pnpm format:check` fails on any file
# checked out with CRLF. Git on Windows does exactly that when `core.autocrlf=true` — the
# default from the Git for Windows installer — which silently rewrote every text file in the
# working tree and broke `format:check` on ~70 files that no one had touched. Without this
# file the breakage returns on the next clone or checkout, because it is produced by the
# checkout itself rather than by anything in the repo.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes the working tree match what the index already stores: Git has
# always normalised these blobs to LF on the way in, so this changes what lands on disk and
# nothing about repository content. It also makes every developer's tree byte-identical
# regardless of their `core.autocrlf`, which is the point.
#
# After adding or changing a rule here, run `git add --renormalize .` to re-apply it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries. Declared rather than left to Git's heuristic, so a PNG whose first bytes happen
# to look like text can never be mangled by end-of-line conversion.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary

# Generated. Excluded from diffs and from language statistics, not from formatting — that is
